    "lawCode": "Amd §103, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6731\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the general municipal law, in relation to authorizing\nschool districts to order from certain farmers without requiring a\nwaiver\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThis bill amends General Municipal Law to allow school districts to\npurchase from associations of ten or fewer producers or growers or to\nmake purchases of less than 25,000.00 without applying for permission\nfrom the Commissioner of Education.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 - amends the General Municipal Law to allow school districts\nto purchase from an association of growers or producers of ten or\nfewer farm owners or to make a purchase of $25,000.00 or less without\napplying for permission from the Commissioner of Education. In the\nsituation the association is greater than ten producers or growers and\nno other producers or growers have offered to sell to the school, then\nthe school district may apply for permission from the Commissioner of\nEducation. In addition, if the purchase is greater than $25,000.00 and\nno other producers or growers have offered to sell to the school, then\nthe school district may apply for permission from the Commissioner of\nEducation.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis bill encourages schools to purchase local food products. Not only\ndoes this help local growers and producers, but it also grants\nstudents access to healthier and fresher foods. Further, it allows\nschools to act in a more socially and environmentally conscious\nmanner, while uniting with the regional community.\n\nNew York State agriculture is primarily comprised of small farms, as\ndefined by the United States Department of Agriculture. This diversity\nof smaller farms and thus smaller product yields means that in order\nto sell to institutions like a school district, the farmers must\ncollaborate to aggregate their products and thus have the higher\nvolume for an extended season that school districts prefer. This\ncooperation is supported by the State of New York. In 2013 New York\nState backed the development of food hubs, which encourage farmers to\nwork together cooperatively to grow and produce food to lower\nproduction and distribution costs. This bill fosters the development\nof our smaller farms by allowing these collaborating family farm\noperations to sell to local school districts by making the process\nclearer and more efficient.\n\nThe current General Municipal Law requires that school districts apply\nfor permission from the Commissioner of Education to purchase food\nsuch as eggs, livestock, fish, or dairy products from separate growers\nand producers if there are more than ten growers or producers in the\nassociation. This application process can be burdensome to smaller\nschool districts and contradicts current food hub programming by\ndiscouraging purchases from cooperatives or food hubs.\n\nCurrent law makes purchasing locally difficult and fails to promote\nthe current farm-to-school program. By amending current law to allow\nschools to make purchases of less than $25,000.00 from groups of\ngrowers and producers of more than ten without applying for\npermission, schools will have easier access to local agricultural\nproducts. This amendment will help New York State continue to meet its\ngoal of maximizing investment in the agricultural sector.\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
